This is an end-to-end benchmarking script for any GenAI-supported ONNX model.


Prerequisites: 
0) Install onnxruntime-genai, pandas, tqdm and psutil (the guidance fast-forward benchmark also needs jsonschema)

1) Use builder.py to build the desired ONNX model

2) Run this script with the desired arguments. Run benchmark_e2e.py -h for help.


Example call to benchmarking script
python benchmark_e2e.py -i {model folder} -b 1 -l 128 -g 256 -r 100 -w 10 -k 5 -o {output csv file name}

Engine guidance benchmark

Build with guidance and run paired guided and unguided requests to compare setup latency, time to first token, inter-token latency, decode throughput, and schema compliance:

python build.py --use_cuda --use_guidance --skip_tests --skip_examples
python benchmark/python/benchmark_engine_guidance.py -m {model folder} -e cuda --warmup 5 --iterations 30 --output guidance-profile.json

Guidance remains request-local. The benchmark alternates guided and unguided requests on one Engine and reports token counts because constrained and unconstrained output lengths may differ.

Engine guidance fast-forward benchmark

Run the same guided turns with guidance fast-forward off and on, on one Engine, alternating which mode runs first each iteration. Turns are greedy, so the two modes can be compared token for token:

python benchmark/python/benchmark_engine_guidance_fast_forward.py -m {model folder} --workloads weather tool_call invoice --requests 8 --iterations 5 --output ff.json

Workloads: weather, tool_call and invoice use fixed-whitespace JSON, which forces the most tokens; weather_flexible, long_text and choice force few. By default every guided turn starts at once. Options:

  --concurrency {n}        keep at most n guided turns in flight (closed loop)
  --arrival_rate {n/s}     start guided turns as a Poisson process instead
  --peers {n}              unguided turns running alongside; --replenish_peers starts a new one whenever one finishes while guided work remains, and --peer_delay_ms {ms} starts them while guided turns are forcing tokens
  --peer_drafts controlled peers propose their own solo output as drafts; by default the model's own drafter, such as DFlash, drafts for them
  --cancel_after {tokens}  cancel each guided turn after that many tokens, to measure cancellation latency
  --event_buffer {n}       drain at most n events per Run call
  --context_sentences {n}  put n sentences of background, unique per turn, before each guided prompt so prefills compete for the step's token budget
  --no_prefix_caching      every iteration repeats the same prompts, so the prefix cache skips most prefill after the first; disable it when prefill work should be measured
  --modes off|on|both      off never calls the fast-forward setter, so the script also runs on a build without it

Latency is measured from when a turn is due, so it includes request creation and grammar compilation.

The JSON reports, for off and on (mean, p50, p95, p99, max where they are distributions):

  model steps, and Run calls (more when a step's events exceed the event buffer)
  draft tokens proposed, evaluated and accepted, for controlled drafts and the model's own drafter alike
  wall time and the guided span (start to last guided finish); guided turns/s and tokens/s use the span, since peers can outlive the guided work; peer tokens/s
  guided arrival-to-first-token, steps to first token and end-to-end latency
  finish reasons and the rate of guided outputs valid against the workload's full JSON schema
  cancellation latency
  peer inter-token gaps, arrival-to-first-token and Run calls before the first token
  overlap_* fields: throughput and peer gaps for only the time when guided turns and peers were both present, with overlap_fraction its share of wall time; conclusions about mixed load hold only for that window
  peers_matching_reference: each peer's output compared with its solo output, and where the others first differ
  build: the onnxruntime_genai version and module that ran

Controlled drafts come from the peer's solo output while verifying those drafts, rerun until it stops changing, because verification can change greedy output; peer_reference reports how many passes that took and whether it converged. A controlled-draft run fails if a scenario evaluates no drafts, so it cannot silently measure ordinary decoding.

Off/on comparison: the summary counts pairs with identical token IDs and identical text and lists where differing pairs first diverge. Fast-forward can change token IDs, so identical text matters more than identical IDs. Batched numerics can also change greedy output between identical runs, so off_repeat and on_repeat compare each iteration with the first one of the same mode as a baseline.

Comparing two builds: run --modes off on each and compare guided_outputs_sha256, steps to first token and the latency fields. This reaches the order in which prompts share spare tokens only when prompts are longer than max_scheduled_tokens allows in one step, and peer admission during prefill only with --peer_delay_ms inside that window; continuation turns, search.chunk_size, recurrent-state models and drafts dropped for cache capacity are covered by the C++ scheduler tests, not this script. Check that each process loaded its own onnxruntime_genai library: the summary's build.module, and the native library the process mapped.

CPU preprocessing/inference contention benchmark (Linux)

Use benchmark_preprocessing_contention.py with a real supported text model export, a supported multimodal image processor export, and an image. The text model must contain real weights, not a dummy test graph. The preprocessing model may be a controlled fixture when only its processor is measured; disclose this when reporting results.

python benchmark/python/benchmark_preprocessing_contention.py --inference-model {real text model} --preprocessing-model {image model or fixture} --image {image file} --image-prompt "<image>Describe this image." --cpus 4,5,6,7 --cpu-counts 4 1 --workers 0 1 3 --seconds 6 --trials 3 --output contention.json

The benchmark uses separate processes sharing the selected CPU affinity, not Python threads competing for the GIL. Both model configurations are temporary CPU-only overlays; the source exports are not modified. Inference uses as many intra-op threads as selected CPUs; preprocessing sessions use one thread. ORT session spinning is disabled. Worker settings are applied before processor creation, and observed worker creation and preprocessing output equality are checked.

Each worker is warmed up. Within each trial, the order of inference-only, preprocessing-only, and simultaneous windows rotates. JSON records inference tokens/second, preprocessing calls/second, and median/p95 operation latency. Token throughput includes prompt processing and generator restarts every 32 tokens; it is not decode-only throughput. Each window runs for at least the requested duration, finishing any in-flight operation. The processes share the CPUs as an application would, but do not share a model instance.

The one-CPU run is an affinity-limited setup, not a cgroup quota experiment. Affinity and worker settings are explicit; the pool default still does not detect process quotas. Results describe CPU resource contention, not end-to-end multimodal inference or universal inference speedups. Run on an otherwise idle allocation and retain all three worker controls; faster isolated preprocessing can still slow concurrent inference.
